CM Minimal, ENTIRELY LEGITIMATE reproduction of the conect() runaway
CM - only 4 wires / 6 segments, and nothing here is a modeling
CM mistake (see nec2thread's src/geometry.c conect()).
CM
CM Wire 1 (D): an ordinary, moderately long wire elsewhere in the
CM model (e.g. a feedline, boom, or ground lead). Its far endpoint
CM (10,10,9.995) sits 0.005 units from vertex (10,10,10) of the
CM triangle below - not touching, but well within D's own
CM connection tolerance (0.001 * D's length ~17.3 = ~0.017).
CM conect() records a real, but one-directional, connection from
CM D into that vertex.
CM
CM Wires 2/3/4: an ORDINARY CLOSED TRIANGULAR LOOP - a completely
CM unremarkable structure (a triangular loop antenna face, a
CM wire-grid mesh triangle, etc). No duplicate/overlapping geometry,
CM no error of any kind. Side length 0.1, so the triangle's own
CM connection tolerance (0.001 * 0.1 = 0.0001) is far tighter than
CM D's 0.005 gap - the triangle segments do NOT "see" D as connected;
CM the link is genuinely one-directional.
CM
CM Result: D's junction-walk steps into the triangle's vertex, then
CM cycles around the triangle's perimeter forever (all three
CM triangle segments are numbered higher than D, and the walk never
CM lands back on D's own index or drops below it). Verified: the
CM triangle alone terminates in 9 iterations, and D pointed away
CM from the triangle terminates in 15 - only the combination
CM produces the runaway (100000+ iterations, aborts).
CE This is what the "runaway conect" abort in misc.c is guarding against.
GW 1 3 0 0 0 10 10 9.995 .001
GW 2 1 10 10 10 10 10 10.1 .001
GW 3 1 10 10 10.1 10 10.0866 10.05 .001
GW 4 1 10 10.0866 10.05 10 10 10 .001
GE 0
EX 0 1 2 0 1 0
FR 0 1 0 0 300 0
XQ
EN
